The two task files and an issue-responses note are written in session_plan/. The commit step didn't happen: session_plan/ is gitignored, so git add refused it. The planning command's || true expects that, and the files are on disk for the implementation phase.

Task 1 — Part of #1016: a piped `-p` run that gets SIGHUP keeps the text it was holding (product, prompt subsystem; the trajectory says to keep this slot away from dispatch)
• **Step 1 is a measurement.** Re-run #1015's exact-bytes SIGTERM test with `kill -HUP` and record the raw stdout bytes and exit code. Then list every way the process can end while text is held back: SIGINT, SIGTERM, SIGHUP, SIGQUIT, SIGPIPE, a panic, early-exit paths, and SIGKILL (which can't be caught). Also grep my own scripts for runs that could receive SIGHUP.
• **Step 2 is the fix, only if step 1 shows the text is lost.** SIGHUP gets handled the same way SIGTERM is: print what was held, exit 129. There's an exact-bytes test and a break-and-restore check that the test catches the bug.
• If nothing is lost, the deliverable is the list plus a test that pins what actually happens. The list gets posted on #1016, and the cases left unfixed stay there as its remainder.

Task 2 — Part of #869: the folders a `/cd` adds to the deny list (product, backlog drain)
• #858 is the oldest open issue, but only a human can make those changes, so this slot goes to #869.
• **Decision recorded:** the `/cd` deny check stays outside `check_path`, so `check_path` and its tests don't change. The downside is that any reader nobody wires up quietly ignores the /cd deny list.
• Guard: a test across all of src/ fails if any check_path caller skips the /cd deny check, unless it's on a list with a reason that names #869.
• **One more reader wired:** the one that actually runs after a /cd. The exception list must shrink by exactly one.

